otification (of a missed call) without incurring fees.", "tags": ["slang", "transitive"], "examples": ["Hey man, prank me when you wanna get picked up.", "I don't have your number in my phone; can you prank me?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["missed call"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, archaic) To adorn in a showy manner; to dress or equip ostentatiously.", "tags": ["archaic", "transitive"], "examples": ["1748, James Thomson, The Castle of Indolence, B:II\nAnd there a Seaſon atween June and May,\nHalf prankt with Spring, with Summer half imbrown'd,\nA liſtleſs Climate made, where, Sooth to ſay,\nNo living Wight could work, ne cared even for Play."], "quotes": [{"text": "In sumptuous tire she ioyd her selfe to prancke", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book II, Canto II”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC, stanza 36:"}, {"text": "Flora, wanton-eyed\nFor birth, and with all flowrets prankt and pied:", "reference": "1880, Dante Gabriel Rosetti, For Spring, by Sandro Botticelli, lines 2–3:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To make an ostentatious show.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "White houses prank where once were huts.", "reference": "1867, Matthew Arnold, “Obermann Once More”, in New Poems:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prank", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}